# Why Decisions, Not Predictions

* Why Traditional ML Falls Short
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson08.1-Causal_AI_intro.smd:318 '* Where ML Falls Short'

- @Problem@: **traditional machine learning** optimizes _prediction accuracy_ on a
  fixed distribution, which ignores four things a decision requires:
  1. _Causality_: causal mechanisms governing the predicted system, focusing only on
     correlation
  2. _Uncertainty_: no quantification of what the model does not know
  3. _Business objective_: a prediction is not a decision
  4. _Dynamics_: the world reacts to the actions informed by the prediction

- @Key idea@: each gap turns an _accurate prediction model_ into a _poor model for
  decision-making_

* From Data Science to Decision Science
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson08.2-Causal_AI_concepts.smd:129 '* From Data Science to Decision Science'

- @Comparison@: **data science** and **decision science** optimize for different
  goals

```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Dimension", "Data Science", "Decision Science"),
  rows: (
    ("Goal", "Predict outcomes", "Choose best actions"),
    ("Question", "What will happen?", "What should we do?"),
    ("Method", "Statistical models, ML", "Causal models, decision theory"),
    ("Output", "Forecast", "Action, uncertainty, expected outcome"),
    ("Metric", "Accuracy, AUC, RMSE", "Business outcome, ROI"),
    ("Data", "Observations", "Observations + causal structure"),
  ),
  bold-first-col: true,
)
```

- @Key idea@: shipping a _prediction_ when the business instead needs a _decision_
  delivers little or no business value, however accurate the prediction is

* Causal vs Predictive Questions
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson08.2-Causal_AI_concepts.smd:161 '* Causal Questions vs. Predictive Questions'

- @Comparison@: every business question has a **predictive form** and a **causal
  form**, and only the latter one says what to do
```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Predictive Question", "Causal Question"),
  rows: (
    ("Which customers will churn?", "What action prevents churn?"),
    ("What will sales be next quarter?", "How would a 10% price cut affect sales?"),
    ("Which students will fail the exam?", "Does tutoring improve exam scores?"),
    ("Which transactions are fraudulent?", "What policy change reduces fraud?"),
  ),
  bold-first-col: false,
)
```

- @Key idea@
  - _Predictive questions_ can be answered from observational data alone, which is
    enough for monitoring and alerting, but not for choosing an action
  - _Causal questions_ require causal models or experiments, and are the only ones
    that support decisions and policy design

* The Analytics Maturity Ladder
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson08.2-Causal_AI_concepts.smd:189 '* A Roadmap From Prediction to Decision Intelligence'

- @Comparison@: organizations climb four levels of **analytical sophistication**

```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Level", "Question asked", "Tools"),
  rows: (
    ("1. Descriptive", "What happened?", "Dashboards, reports"),
    ("2. Predictive", "What will happen?", "ML, forecasting"),
    ("3. Causal", "Why? What if we act?", "DAGs, do-calculus"),
    ("4. Decision", "What should we do?", "Causal models + utility"),
  ),
  bold-first-col: true,
)
```

- @Problem@: most organizations are stuck at _Level 2_, predicting well but unable to
  say what to do

- @Key idea@: climbing to _Levels 3_ and _4_ is the jump this book makes, from
  prediction pipelines to decision pipelines

# The Cost of Ignoring Causality

## When Correlation Misleads

* Correlation Encodes Confounding as Causation
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson08.1-Causal_AI_intro.smd:345 '* The Cost of Ignoring Causality (1/2)'
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson11.1-Decision_Making_with_Causal_Models.smd:161 '* Confounding: The Root Cause'
// From: book_springer/related_papers.md:12 '- [P007] Zhang et al., "A Survey on Causal Inference", 2020'

- @Problem@: a purely **predictive model** learns $\Pr(Y | X)$ from historical data
  and absorbs any _association_ in the data, _spurious or not_
  [@yao2020survey]
  - It answers _"what will happen?"_, but not _"what should we do?"_
  - It assumes the world stays fixed, even though the actions informed by the
    prediction themselves will change the world

::: columns
:::: {.column width=55%}
- @Example@: ice cream sales and drowning deaths are highly correlated
  - Prediction: _"more ice cream $\to$ more drownings"_ (which is accurate)
  - Decision built on it: _"ban ice cream to cut drownings"_ (which is disastrous)
  - Confounder: hot weather drives both
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}
```graphviz
digraph IceCreamDrowning {
  bgcolor="transparent";
  pad="0.15";
  splines=spline;
  nodesep=0.30;
  ranksep=0.50;
  rankdir=TB;

  node [shape=box,
        style="rounded,filled",
        penwidth=1.8,
        fontname="Helvetica",
        fontsize=12,
        margin="0.22,0.14",
        height=0.50];

  edge [color="#A3B1C0",
        penwidth=1.3,
        arrowhead=vee,
        arrowsize=0.75,
        fontname="Helvetica",
        fontsize=10,
        fontcolor="#7B8794"];

  H [label=<<b>Hot Weather</b><br/><font point-size="10" color="#6B4517">Confounder</font>>,
     fillcolor="#FBEBD4", color="#D9A85F", fontcolor="#6B4517"];
  I [label=<<b>Ice Cream Sales</b><br/><font point-size="10" color="#1F4E79">Observed</font>>,
     fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
  D [label=<<b>Drownings</b><br/><font point-size="10" color="#6B2A44">Outcome</font>>,
     fillcolor="#F6E1E8", color="#D98CA8", fontcolor="#6B2A44"];

  { rank=same; I; D; }

  H -> I [label="  drives"];
  H -> D [label="  drives"];
  I -> D [style=dashed, penwidth=1.6, color="#C0455B", fontcolor="#C0455B", label="  spurious", constraint=false];
}
```
::::
:::

- @Key idea@:
  - The observational and interventional quantities disagree
    $$\Pr(Y | X) \neq \Pr(Y | do(X))$$
    [@pearl2009causality]
  - Acting on $\Pr(Y | X)$ is what makes correlation-based decisions backfire

* Missing Interventions and Counterfactuals
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson08.1-Causal_AI_intro.smd:113 '* What ML Systems Cannot Tell You'

- @Problem@: a correlation model answers _"what is?"_, never _"what if?"_ or
  _"why?"_
  - Two classes of business question stay permanently out of reach
    [@rubin1974estimating]:
    - Intervention questions: _"If we cut price 10%, how do units sold and revenue
      change?"_
    - Counterfactual questions: _"Would this customer have churned had we offered a
      discount?"_

- @Example@: a tutoring program correlates with higher exam scores, but:
  - _"Does tutoring cause the improvement?"_, or
  - _"Do strong students self-select into it?"_
  - No amount of additional observational data resolves the question

- @Key idea@: answering these questions requires a causal model, not a more
  accurate predictor

* Selection Bias and the Missing Counterfactual
// From:

- @Problem@: historical data records only the decisions actually made, never
  the alternative
  - E.g., loan data shows outcomes for approved applicants, but not always data
    for rejected ones
  - **Confounding biases** the _relationship_ between variables
    that were recorded
  - **Selection bias** comes from which units got recorded at all, a problem baked
    into the data before any model is fit

- @Example@: a credit model trained only on approved loans learns what
  predicts repayment among people the bank already trusted
  - It cannot say who among the rejected applicants would have repaid

- @Key idea@: a model can be accurate on the population it observed and
  still be blind to the population the business needed to reason about

## Structural Failure Modes

* Interference and Spillovers Across Units
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson08.7-Causal_experiments.smd:315 '* Cannibalization and Interference'

- @Problem@: standard ML assumes each unit's outcome depends only on its own
  treatment
  - Marketplaces, pricing equilibria, and social networks routinely violate
    this assumption

- @Example@: a discount shown only to group A can cannibalize purchases from
  group B
  - The two groups share the same limited supply or the same social network
  - Group B is no longer a clean counterfactual for group A

- @Key idea@: when one unit's action changes another unit's outcome, the
  usual estimate of a treatment effect is biased, however accurate the
  underlying predictor is

* Simpson's Paradox
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson08.1-Causal_AI_intro.smd:345 '* The Cost of Ignoring Causality (1/2)'
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson11.1-Decision_Making_with_Causal_Models.smd:86 '* Simpson's Paradox'
// From: book_springer/related_papers.md:3 '- [P005] Pearl, "Simpson's Paradox, Confounding, and Collapsibility", 1999'

- @Definition@: the **Simpson's paradox** is a trend within every subgroup
  reverses once the subgroups are pooled [@pearl2009causality]

- @Example@: a drug's recovery rate
```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Group", "Recovery w/ drug", "Recovery w/o drug"),
  rows: (
    ("Male", "81 / 87 = 93%", "234 / 270 = 87%"),
    ("Female", "192 / 263 = 73%", "55 / 80 = 69%"),
    ("Combined", "273 / 350 = 78%", "289 / 350 = 83%"),
  ),
  bold-first-col: true,
)
```

```graphviz
digraph SimpsonsParadox {
  bgcolor="transparent";
  pad="0.15";
  splines=spline;
  nodesep=0.30;
  ranksep=0.50;
  rankdir=LR;

  node [shape=box,
        style="rounded,filled",
        penwidth=1.8,
        fontname="Helvetica",
        fontsize=12,
        margin="0.22,0.14",
        height=0.50];

  edge [color="#A3B1C0",
        penwidth=1.3,
        arrowhead=vee,
        arrowsize=0.75,
        fontname="Helvetica",
        fontsize=10,
        fontcolor="#7B8794"];

  G [label=<<b>Gender</b><br/><font point-size="10" color="#6B4517">Confounder</font>>,
     fillcolor="#FBEBD4", color="#D9A85F", fontcolor="#6B4517"];
  T [label=<<b>Drug Choice</b><br/><font point-size="10" color="#1F4E79">Treatment</font>>,
     fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
  R [label=<<b>Recovery</b><br/><font point-size="10" color="#6B2A44">Outcome</font>>,
     fillcolor="#F6E1E8", color="#D98CA8", fontcolor="#6B2A44"];

  G -> T [label="  assigns"];
  G -> R [label="  drives"];
  T -> R [label="  true effect", penwidth=1.6, color="#1F4E79", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
}
```

- The drug helps _each_ subgroup, yet appears to hurt when the groups are
  pooled
  - Gender confounds both treatment assignment and the outcome: females
    receive the drug far more often (263 of 350) than males (87 of 350), and
    recover less easily than males regardless of treatment
  - Pooling stacks a mostly-female drug group against a mostly-male no-drug
    group, so the harder-to-treat gender dominates the "drug" column and
    reverses the sign of an effect that helps everyone

- @Key idea@: a model can look correct in aggregate and be wrong, or
  harmful, for every segment it is applied to

* Berkson's Paradox
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson08.4-Causal_networks.smd:1147 '* Collider Bias'

- @Definition@: a **collider** is a variable influenced by two or more
  independent causes

- @Fact@: Conditioning on a collider opens a spurious association between its
  causes, a mechanism known as Berkson's paradox

- @Example@: statistics skill and flattery both influence who gets promoted
  - Among everyone, statistics skill and flattery are independent
  - Among the _promoted_ only, they become negatively associated: bad at
    statistics implies must be a good flatterer

// TODO(ai_gp): Add another example and explain
// TODO(gp): Add reference to Pearl's book about how many studies are wrong

- @Key idea@: conditioning on the wrong variable, not just omitting the
  right one, is enough to manufacture a correlation with no causal meaning

* Discarding Domain Knowledge and Mechanisms
// From:
// From: book_springer/related_papers.md:71 '- [P001] Ribeiro et al., "Why Should I Trust You? Explaining the Predictions of Any Classifier", 2016'

- @Problem@: a purely correlation-based pipeline discards known structure,
  physics, economics, or the causal graph, in favor of whatever pattern the
  data happens to contain
  - No principled way to inject constraints or priors

::: columns
:::: {.column width=55%}
- @Example@: a wolf-vs-husky classifier with high test accuracy keys on
  _snow_ in the background, not on the animal [@ribeiro2016why]
  - Accurate for the wrong reason, and worthless once conditions change
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}
```graphviz
digraph WolfHusky {
  bgcolor="transparent";
  pad="0.15";
  splines=spline;
  nodesep=0.30;
  ranksep=0.50;
  rankdir=LR;

  node [shape=box,
        style="rounded,filled",
        penwidth=1.8,
        fontname="Helvetica",
        fontsize=12,
        margin="0.22,0.14",
        height=0.50];

  edge [color="#A3B1C0",
        penwidth=1.3,
        arrowhead=vee,
        arrowsize=0.75,
        fontname="Helvetica",
        fontsize=10,
        fontcolor="#7B8794"];

  Species [label=<<b>True Species</b><br/><font point-size="10" color="#2E5A3D">Wolf vs Husky</font>>,
           fillcolor="#DFEDE0", color="#8FB79A", fontcolor="#2E5A3D"];
  Snow    [label=<<b>Snow in Background</b><br/><font point-size="10" color="#6B2A44">Spurious feature</font>>,
           fillcolor="#F6E1E8", color="#D98CA8", fontcolor="#6B2A44"];
  Pred    [label=<<b>Classifier Prediction</b><br/><font point-size="10" color="#1F4E79">Model output</font>>,
           fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];

  Species -> Pred [style=dashed, label="  ignored"];
  Snow -> Pred [label="  used instead", penwidth=1.6, color="#C0455B", fontcolor="#C0455B"];
}
```
::::
:::

- @Key idea@: a model that ignores mechanism inherits every artifact of how
  the training data was collected

# The Cost of Ignoring Uncertainty

* Point Estimates Without Error Bars
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson11.1-Decision_Making_with_Causal_Models.smd:1217 '* Communicating Uncertainty to Stakeholders'

- @Problem@: most business problems are "small data" problems with large
  uncertainty, yet standard ML pipelines emit a single number as if it were
  certain
  - No error bars, no posterior, no sense of how wrong the estimate could be

- @Example@: a demand forecast of _"1,200 units next month"_ hides whether the
  true range is 1,100-1,300 or 400-3,000
  - The two ranges call for different inventory decisions

- @Example@: a prediction that the price of a stock will go up 1%, has
  implies different actions depending on the prediction uncertainty
  - For 1 +/- 2% at 95% confidence, ignore the prediction
  - For 1 +/- 0.01% at 95% confidence, buy

- @Key idea@: a decision-maker needs the spread of an estimate at least as
  much as its center

* Epistemic vs Aleatoric Uncertainty Conflated
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson11.1-Decision_Making_with_Causal_Models.smd:1076 '* Aleatoric vs Epistemic Uncertainty'

- @Problem@: standard models collapse two different kinds of uncertainty
  into one number
  - _Aleatoric_ uncertainty is irreducible randomness in the outcome itself
    - E.g., the outcome of a fair coin flip
  - _Epistemic_ uncertainty comes from not having seen enough data, or from
    the wrong model
    - E.g., a rare disease with only 10 recorded cases

- Without separating them, a model cannot flag what it has never seen: it can
  exhibit maximal confidence off-distribution, precisely where it should
  exhibit maximal uncertainty

- @Example@: a fraud model's low predicted risk for a novel transaction
  pattern may reflect
  - Genuine low risk (aleatoric) or
  - Simply that the model has never seen this pattern before (epistemic)

- @Key idea@: only epistemic uncertainty shrinks with more data; treating
  both as the same number misleads the decision of when to collect more

* No Abstention: Systems That Never Say "I Don't Know"
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson11.2-Probabilistic_deep_learning.smd:1879 '* Predictive Uncertainty in Classification and Regression'

- @Problem@: a standard classifier or regressor always returns an answer,
  even when the input falls far outside its training distribution
  - No abstention or deferral mechanism

- @Example@: a loan-approval model forced to score an applicant profile
  unlike anything in its training data still returns a confident approval or
  denial, while it should route uncertain cases to a human

- @Key idea@: a decision system that cannot say _"I don't know"_ cannot be
  trusted with high-stakes decisions

* Overfitting and the Statistical Significance Traps
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson08.7-Causal_experiments.smd:270 '* Peeking and the Multiple Comparisons Trap'

- @Problem@: without a prior or assumptions to constrain the fit, nothing
  bounds overfitting

- Statistical significance traps compound the problem:
  - _P-hacking_: checking a test repeatedly and stopping the moment $p < 0.05$
    inflates the true false-positive rate well past the nominal $5\%$
  - _Multiple comparisons_: testing many metrics at once guarantees a spurious
    win somewhere
  - _Burning the test set_: reusing the same held-out data across many rounds
    of tuning turns it into training data in disguise

- @Key idea@: overfitting is not a single mistake in one run, it accumulates
  from every repeated look at the same data

# The Cost of Ignoring the Business Objective

## Proxies, Costs, and Trade-offs

* Optimizing the Wrong Objective
// From:
// From: book_springer/related_papers.md:131 '- [P096] Goodhart, "Problems of Monetary Management: The U.K. Experience", 1984'

- @Problem@: models chase a proxy metric (e.g., clicks or accuracy) instead of
  the business goal it stands in for (e.g., revenue, retention, or wellbeing)

- @Definition@: **Goodhart's law** states that once a proxy becomes a target,
  it stops measuring what you care about [@goodhart1984problems]

- @Example@: a recommender optimized purely for click-through rate learns to
  surface sensational content, growing clicks while eroding long-term
  engagement and trust

- @Key idea@: the objective a model is trained on becomes the objective the
  business actually gets, whether or not that was the intent

* Symmetric Losses for Asymmetric Errors
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson07.2-Posterior_Based_Decisions.smd:96 '* Loss Function: Motivation'

- @Problem@: standard losses such as log-loss and mean squared error treat
  every error the same size
  - Business errors are not symmetric

- @Example@:
  - the cost of a false negative for stock return prediction should not be
    symmetric since there is a limit to how many consecutive losses, but not a
    limit to consecutive wins

- @Example@:
    - A fraud false negative and a fraud false positive cost differently
    - A fraud model with 95% accuracy that blocks half of legitimate
      transactions is a costly decision system, however accurate it is by the
      standard metric

- @Key idea@: cost asymmetry has to be built into the objective, not bolted
  in subsequent stages of a decision pipeline, or bolted on after deployment as
  an afterthought

* Multi-Objective Decisions Under Constraints
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson11.1-Decision_Making_with_Causal_Models.smd:475 '* Multi-Criteria Trade-offs'

- @Problem@: real decisions trade off competing goals under budget,
  capacity, and legal limits
  - A single scalar loss cannot express a constrained, multi-stakeholder
    trade-off

- @Example@: a hospital choosing a cancer therapy weighs five-year survival,
  quality of life, and cost simultaneously
  - No single "best" therapy exists independent of how those three are
    weighed against each other

- @Key idea@: collapsing a multi-objective decision into one loss function
  hides the trade-off instead of making it explicit

## From Scores to Actionable Decisions

* Black-Box Scores Are Not Recommendations
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson08.1-Causal_AI_intro.smd:202 '* Problem 3: ML Explainability'
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson13.1-Explainability.smd:532 '* Counterfactual Explanations'
// From: book_springer/related_papers.md:71 '- [P001] Ribeiro et al., "Why Should I Trust You? Explaining the Predictions of Any Classifier", 2016'

- @Problem@: a black-box score ranks units but names no lever and gives no
  _why_ [@ribeiro2016why]

- @Example@:
  - A churn score does not say which action prevents churn
  - Instead of _"this customer has churn score 0.8"_, a useful recommendation
    says _"offering a discount lowers this customer's churn probability by 12
    points"_
  - It names the lever, the effect, and the unit it acts on

- @Fact@: credit, hiring, and healthcare increasingly require explanations, not
  just scores, to justify decisions to regulators and stakeholders

- @Key idea@: a recommendation needs an actionable lever and an explanation,
  neither of which a bare prediction provides

* The Cost of Solving Sub-Problems Separately
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson02.2-ML_Paradigms.smd:326 '* How Are Machine Learning Systems Organized?'

- @Problem@: a decision pipeline is often split into smaller, individually
  optimized pieces, e.g., a prediction model followed by a separate rule that
  turns predictions into actions

- @Example@: an inventory system that forecasts demand accurately, then
  applies a fixed reorder rule, is likely to perform worse than a system trained
  to minimize inventory cost directly

- @Fact@: End-to-end machine learning has been shown to outperform stitching
  together separately-optimized parts
  - This applies also how to decision-making systems

- @Key idea@: optimizing each piece in isolation does not add up to
  optimizing the decision

# The Cost of Ignoring Dynamics and Feedback

## A World That Reacts

* The Static-World Assumption Breaks
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson08.1-Causal_AI_intro.smd:274 '* Problem 6: Distribution Shift'

- @Problem@: any decision changes the world, breaking the distribution the
  model was trained on

- Three ways the world moves out from under a static model:
  - _Concept shift_: the link $\Pr(Y | X)$ between inputs and outcomes changes
  - _Covariate shift_: the input distribution $\Pr(X)$ changes while the
    relationship stays the same
  - _Label shift_: the outcome distribution $\Pr(Y)$ itself changes

- @Example@: a card-fraud model trained on last year's transactions
  - _Concept shift_: attackers adopt new tactics, so a transaction pattern
    that used to be safe now signals fraud, changing $\Pr(Y | X)$
  - _Covariate shift_: contactless payments become far more common, changing
    $\Pr(X)$ even though fraud tactics on that channel are unchanged
  - _Label shift_: a data breach leaks card numbers and the overall fraud
    rate itself rises, changing $\Pr(Y)$
  - The model has no way to detect any of the three on its own

- @Example@: a house-price model trained on pre-pandemic listings
  - Remote work permanently changes what commute distance is worth (concept
    shift), while a shift to lower interest rates raises average sale prices
    across the board (label shift)

- @Key idea@: a model that encodes (causal) mechanisms, not just patterns,
  degrades more gracefully because mechanisms are more stable across
  distribution shift than surface correlations

* Feedback Loops and Bias Amplification
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson08.1-Causal_AI_intro.smd:252 '* Problem 5: Feedback Loops'
// From: book_springer/related_papers.md:73 '- [P003] Taori et al., "Data Feedback Loops: Model-driven Amplification of Dataset Biases", 2020'

- @Problem@: predictions act on the world and reshape the future data the
  model will be retrained on [@taori2023data]

- @Example@:
  - A recommender that surfaces sensational content earns more clicks on that
    content
  - Future training data contains even more clicks on sensational content
  - The next model recommends more of it still

- @Example@:
  - Over-policing a neighborhood produces more arrests there
  - The arrest data trains a model that flags the neighborhood as higher risk
  - This new data justifies more policing

- @Key idea@: a feedback loop turns a small initial bias into a growing one,
  purely from the model training on the outcomes of its own past decisions

* Performativity: When the Prediction Changes the Outcome
// From:
// From: book_springer/related_papers.md:75 '- [P099] Liu et al., "Performative Prediction", 2021'

- @Definition@: **performativity** is a prediction that changes the very
  quantity it predicts [@perdomo2020performative]

- @Example@: a published credit-risk score moves borrower behavior, which
  moves the true default rate the score was trying to predict

- @Example@: a navigation app that predicts a route as fastest routes many
  drivers onto it at once, congesting the road until it is no longer the
  fastest option, invalidating the very prediction that attracted them

- @Key idea@: once a prediction is performative, $\Pr(Y | X)$ is no longer a
  fixed target to fit, it is a function of which model gets deployed

## Missing Exploration and Long Horizons

* No Exploration: Trapped in the Logged Policy
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson08.7-Causal_experiments.smd:572 '* Policy Evaluation and Off-Policy Learning'

- @Problem@: an observational-only model never experiments, so it stays
  trapped inside the support of whatever policy logged the training data
  [@bottou2013counterfactual]
  - It cannot learn the effect of an action nobody has ever taken, an
    overlap, or positivity, violation

- @Example@: a pricing model trained on historical prices that never
  dropped below \$20 cannot say what happens at \$15, no matter how much
  historical data it has

- @Key idea@: some business questions cannot be answered by more historical
  data, only by a deliberate intervention

* Delayed and Long-Horizon Effects
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson08.7-Causal_experiments.smd:492 '* When A/B Testing Is Not Enough'

- @Problem@: a model optimized for an immediate proxy ignores downstream
  outcomes and has no way to assign credit to actions over time

- @Example@: a support team optimized for fast ticket closure closes
  tickets quickly, whether or not the underlying issue is resolved
  - The true cost, repeat complaints and lost trust, shows up months later,
    in a different metric

- @Example@: a collections agency optimized to maximize dollars recovered
  this quarter pushes aggressive repayment plans that borrowers later
  default on
  - The extra recovery this quarter is repaid, months later, with a lower
    lifetime repayment rate and reputational damage

- @Key idea@: short-term proxies and long-term business outcomes can point
  in opposite directions, and only one of them is what the business actually
  cares about

* Strategic and Adversarial Response
// From:

- @Problem@: once a model is deployed, the people it scores learn to
  optimize against it
  - Standard ML assumes passive agents; in business, agents are not passive

- @Example@: publishing the features of a credit-scoring model invites
  credit gaming; publishing a search-ranking algorithm invites
  keyword-stuffing

- @Example@: a tax-audit model that flags returns above a reported-income
  threshold causes self-employed filers to bunch their reported income just
  below that threshold once the rule becomes known

- @Key idea@: a model trained on how agents behaved before deployment can
  become wrong the moment agents learn how it scores them

# Why This Matters

## Roadmap

* Why AI Projects Fail
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson08.3-Causal_AI_in_business.smd:24 '* Why AI Projects Fail?'

- @Components@: most AI projects fail not because of failures at the
  modeling phase, but because they frame the problems incorrectly
  - _Misalignment_: the model optimizes a proxy, not the business goal
  - _Black boxes_: outputs cannot be explained, so they are not trusted or
    adopted
  - _No lever_: a score is delivered where an action was needed
  - _No feedback_: performance is never monitored after deployment

- @Key idea@: any accurate model that answers the wrong question is a failed
  project, however good its metrics look on a dashboard

* The Root Cause of the Four Costs

- The four costs surveyed in this chapter (i.e., ignoring causality,
  uncertainty, the business objective, and dynamics) share a single root
  cause

- Each one comes from optimizing prediction accuracy on a fixed distribution
  instead of decision quality on a distribution the decision itself changes

- This book shows how to solve all the 4 problems using causal and
  probabilistic approaches to build

- Every technique plugs into one loop

```graphviz
digraph DecisionLoop {
  bgcolor="transparent";
  pad="0.15";
  splines=spline;
  nodesep=0.30;
  ranksep=0.50;
  rankdir=TB;

  node [shape=box,
        style="rounded,filled",
        penwidth=1.8,
        fontname="Helvetica",
        fontsize=12,
        margin="0.22,0.14",
        height=0.50];

  edge [color="#3A4A5C",
        penwidth=1.6,
        arrowhead=vee,
        arrowsize=0.85,
        fontname="Helvetica",
        fontsize=11,
        fontcolor="#3A4A5C"];

  Data     [label=<<b>Data</b><br/><font point-size="10" color="#6B4517">Observations</font>>,
            fillcolor="#FBEBD4", color="#D9A85F", fontcolor="#6B4517"];
  Model    [label=<<b>Model</b><br/><font point-size="10" color="#1F4E39">Learned mechanism</font>>,
            fillcolor="#B7DDD0", color="#6FA890", fontcolor="#1F4E39"];
  Policy   [label=<<b>Policy</b><br/><font point-size="10" color="#1F4E79">Decision rule</font>>,
            fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
  Action   [label=<<b>Action</b><br/><font point-size="10" color="#6B2A44">Enacted choice</font>>,
            fillcolor="#F6E1E8", color="#D98CA8", fontcolor="#6B2A44"];
  Feedback [label=<<b>Feedback</b><br/><font point-size="10" color="#1F4E56">Observed outcome</font>>,
            fillcolor="#C7ECF0", color="#7CC6D0", fontcolor="#1F4E56"];

  Data -> Model -> Policy -> Action -> Feedback;
  Feedback -> Data [label="  retrain / adapt", style=dashed, constraint=false];
}
```

* Book Roadmap

- @Components@: the rest of the book builds the pipeline that proposes
  solutions for the problem highlighted in this chapter
  - _Part II: Advanced Modeling Theory & Tools_ supplies the machinery:
    knowledge representation, probabilistic ML (Bayesian inference,
    uncertainty), and causal ML (SCMs, do-calculus, causal discovery)
  - _Part III: Data_ turns domain knowledge into causal DAGs and causal
    data pipelines, and confronts selection bias and distribution shift
  - _Part IV: Decision-Making Theory & Tools_ turns causal models into
    actions: decision theory, the taxonomy of decision problems, simple
    decisions (bandits, RL), complex decisions (policy gradients,
    multi-agent), and agentic causal reasoning
  - _Part V: Implementation, Deployment & Governance_ covers stakeholder
    alignment, deployment/monitoring/adaptation, and trust, explainability,
    fairness, and governance

